   "text": "The image shows gadobenate dimeglumine, which has three components: two meglumine cations, the gadolinium chelate ligand, and Gd\u00b3\u207a. I read each part from the drawing.\n\n**Meglumine (drawn twice).** It is a six-carbon chain: CH3\u2013NH\u2013CH2, then four CH(OH) carbons, then CH2OH. The stereocentres are marked by hashed and wedged OH bonds. Working from the N end, I assigned each centre from the drawn geometry:\n- C2 (OH hashed): `[C@H]`\n- C3 (OH hashed): `[C@@H]`\n- C4 (OH hashed): `[C@H]`\n- C5 (OH wedged): `[C@H]`\n\nThis gives `CNC[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO`, which matches N-methyl-D-glucamine. As drawn, the amine is neutral, so I wrote it that way.\n\n**Ligand.** A diethylenetriamine backbone carries five arms on its three nitrogens.\n- Four of the arms are CH2COO groups. Two are drawn as carboxylates (O\u207b) and two as COOH.\n- The fifth arm is on a terminal nitrogen. It is a CH(COO\u207b) carbon bearing CH2\u2013O\u2013CH2\u2013phenyl, a benzyloxymethyl group. This carbon has plain bonds in the drawing, so I gave it no stereo.\n- Counting the carboxylate anions gives three O\u207b groups, balancing the Gd\u00b3\u207a.\n\n**Metal.** `[Gd+3]`\n\nSMILES: CNC[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO.CNC[C@H](O)[C@@H](O)[C@H](O)[C@H](O)CO.[O-]C(=O)C(COCc1ccccc1)N(CC(=O)O)CCN(CC(=O)[O-])CCN(CC(=O)[O-])CC(=O)O.[Gd+3]"
